    "reading": {
        "body": "Privacy and AI hiring in this sample is a legal and governance function, not an engineering one: four of five postings are counsel, advisor, or program-management roles.\n\nThe five postings come from four companies and were collected in the weeks before mid-September 2026. Harvey listed the same EMEA counsel title twice, in Dublin and London, which reads as one opening offered in two locations rather than two hires. The rest sit at neoshare in München, OpenAI in San Francisco, and Figure in San Jose. Three of the five are European. Only one, a research engineer role at OpenAI, is a technical build position.\n\nThat shape matters because privacy work scales with deployment, not with compute. Someone has to decide what personal data a model may hold and who approves its use, and that decision is made by lawyers and program managers rather than by the people buying accelerators. Nothing in these listings touches data centers, power, or hardware, so they say nothing about the physical constraints on AI scaling. They describe a governance layer that grows alongside products.\n\nFive postings are a snapshot, not a market. There is no salary, seniority, or applicant volume here, and no earlier period to compare against. The posting dates run from July to September 2026, so the set mixes roles open for weeks with roles listed days before collection. It cannot show whether this kind of hiring is rising or falling.",
